{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>At the opening sin crouches, and toward you is its desire.</b> From here there is support for the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Eruvin 19a) who said that the wicked, even at the very entrance of Gehinnom, do not turn back in repentance. For the opening of Gehinnom is called 'the opening of sin,' for it is an opening and a source opened up for sin and for impurity, and even at the time when a man lies at the opening of sin — that is, the entrance of Gehinnom — nevertheless 'toward you is its desire': the desire of the evil impulse does not cease." "Another matter: because our Sages of blessed memory said (Berakhot 5a), 'A person should always stir up his good impulse against his evil impulse … if it goes, well; if not, let him remind it of the day of death, and then surely it goes.' This is what it says: 'If you do well' — to drive away the evil impulse, then HaShem will lift up His face to you and receive you in repentance. And if you do not do well, so that the evil impulse does not go away, its impurity still within him, then 'at the opening sin crouches' — that is to say, remind it of the day of death, as though the opening of your grave lies open before you, and then it will crouch beneath the burden of sin, that is, the evil impulse that causes you to sin. And even though 'its desire is toward you,' nevertheless 'you shall rule over it.'" "Another matter: because we find that our Sages of blessed memory (Berakhot 61a) compared the evil impulse to a fly, and they supported it from the verse (Ecclesiastes 10:1), 'Dead flies make [the perfumer's ointment] turn rancid,' etc. And the reason for this is that every fly — the power of its mouth is weak, and it has no strength to make a puncture in whole flesh; but in a place where the fly finds an opening in the living flesh, there it crouches, and it has the power in its mouth to widen the breach. So too the evil impulse: its power is weak to make a breach in the living flesh of the righteous many times, for the righteous in all his ways, who has not opened any opening at all to his impulse — it is impossible for it to break through his fence and enter into him to cause him to sin. But at a time when it finds people of his generation who breach even a small breach and open an opening for it to sin and to defile, then it is in its power to widen the breach and add transgression upon sin, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Shabbat 104a), 'One who comes to defile — doors are opened to him,' and some have the reading 'they open for him,' meaning that one opens an opening to the evil impulse so that it finds a place to enter through that breach and to widen it until its blow rises beyond healing. The parable of this: one who finds an opening shut tight on all sides — it is hard for him to open it; but one who finds the opening slightly ajar — it is easy for him to widen the opening until he makes it like a gate in himself, through which everything enters easily. And this is a fitting parable for the evil impulse." "And in this manner we find in the Yalkut, at the end of parashat Beshalaḥ, that it compares Amalek to a fly: just as a fly is greedy for a wound, so Amalek was greedy for the blood of Israel, etc. And why did they compare him specifically to a fly? But because he too could not engage Israel as long as they were whole with HaShem — except at Rephidim, where they slackened their hands from the Torah; then they opened for him an opening, and Amalek came and defiled them through illicit relations, according to the opinion of our Sages of blessed memory (Tanhuma, Tetzeh 9). This is what it concludes there in the midrash: a parable of a man who surrounded his vineyard and said, 'Anyone who breaches the fence, the dog will bite him,' etc. It specifies specifically 'one who breaches a fence' because this is the matter of the opening of the opening that we mentioned, for through the opening many breach through it. So too, through the opening that one opens for his impulse, many accusers enter into him, preventing him from the straight path, as will be explained further in parashat Beshalaḥ. And in tractate Yoma (39a) our Sages of blessed memory said: 'If a man defiles himself a little, they defile him much,' etc. And this is explained according to our approach. And this is what it says, 'At the opening sin crouches,' for the evil impulse, which is called 'sin' — in a place where it finds an opening opened for sin, there it crouches and enters through that opening. And do not say that its desire is only toward the opening alone, but 'toward you is its desire' — all its desire is to proceed from there onward toward you, literally, to draw you away from HaShem. However, as long as it is at the opening of the eyes — that is, at the beginning of its coming toward you — 'you shall rule over it,' in the manner that I explained above on the verse 'he shall strike your head.' But if it has already passed beyond the opening — meaning the small breach — and has already passed over to you literally, to draw away all your essence from HaShem, then it will be difficult for you to rule over it. Therefore the wise man's eyes are in his head, not to give it any place to enter at all, and this is a precious interpretation."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:14.696139540Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}